1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,713
A heat exchanger passes heat between fluids

2
00:00:02,713 --> 00:00:05,174
that never touch — like a car radiator.

3
00:00:05,474 --> 00:00:07,478
Flow direction decides how much.

4
00:00:07,978 --> 00:00:09,909
No new metal. No extra pumping.

5
00:00:09,909 --> 00:00:12,340
So where does the extra heat come from?

6
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:15,206
Packing many tubes into one shell gives

7
00:00:15,206 --> 00:00:17,329
the heat a lot of surface to cross.

8
00:00:17,629 --> 00:00:19,721
Every bit of it has to push through metal

9
00:00:19,721 --> 00:00:21,610
— so something has to do the pushing.

10
00:00:21,910 --> 00:00:24,318
Strip it down to one tube to see what that is.

11
00:00:24,618 --> 00:00:26,852
First, the arrangement that looks natural.

12
00:00:27,352 --> 00:00:29,101
The two streams chase each other

13
00:00:29,101 --> 00:00:30,631
toward the same temperature.

14
00:00:30,931 --> 00:00:32,857
That gap is the driving force —

15
00:00:32,857 --> 00:00:34,844
at the inlet, heat pours across.

16
00:00:35,144 --> 00:00:37,381
By the far end the push is almost gone,

17
00:00:37,381 --> 00:00:39,504
so that stretch of tube barely works.

18
00:00:39,804 --> 00:00:42,421
Keep everything else fixed. Change one thing.

19
00:00:42,921 --> 00:00:43,879
Now — the flip.

20
00:00:46,421 --> 00:00:48,005
The fluids still never mix.

21
00:00:48,005 --> 00:00:49,941
Only where they meet has changed.

22
00:00:50,241 --> 00:00:53,163
The hot stream leaves 14 °C cooler than before.

23
00:00:53,463 --> 00:00:55,090
And the cold stream now leaves

24
00:00:55,090 --> 00:00:56,825
hotter than the hot stream does.

25
00:00:57,125 --> 00:00:59,556
Each one exits at the other's inlet end —

26
00:00:59,556 --> 00:01:01,868
so at every point, hot is still hotter.

27
00:01:02,368 --> 00:01:03,998
In one, the push fades out.

28
00:01:04,298 --> 00:01:05,938
In the other, it never does.

29
00:01:06,238 --> 00:01:08,545
Every metre of tube keeps pulling its weight

30
00:01:08,545 --> 00:01:10,695
— that's where the extra heat comes from.

31
00:01:11,195 --> 00:01:13,273
Same hardware, more heat recovered

32
00:01:13,273 --> 00:01:15,291
— just by choosing the direction.

33
00:01:15,591 --> 00:01:17,869
Multi-pass units blend both patterns

34
00:01:17,869 --> 00:01:19,958
— that's a story for another day.

35
00:01:20,458 --> 00:01:22,915
But tubes don't stay clean. Next time —

36
00:01:22,915 --> 00:01:25,876
what a thin layer of grime does to all of this.
